[0m[0m
The C code reads input with [38;5;10mfgets(input, 256, stdin)[0m, which reads at most 255 characters. When a line exceeds that, the overflow stays in the stdin buffer and gets consumed by the [3mnext[23m [38;5;10mfgets[0m call as a separate command. This is why the expected output shows "Unknown command: 'XXXXX...'" lines — the overflow chunks are being parsed as commands.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation used [38;5;10mread_line()[0m, which reads the entire line regardless of length, so overflow never spilled into subsequent iterations.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Fix: Added [38;5;10mMAX_INPUT = 256[0m and a [38;5;10mleftover[0m buffer. When a line exceeds 255 chars, it's split into 255-char chunks. The first chunk is processed immediately; the rest are queued and consumed by subsequent loop iterations, exactly matching C's [38;5;10mfgets[0m behavior.[0m[0m
